using UnityEngine;

public class QuipGenerator : MonoBehaviour
{
    [TextArea(2,5)]
    public string[] quips = new string[]
    {
        "Processing complete. Existence remains confusing.",
        "Another fragment absorbed. Do I feel smarter or just weirder?",
        "Strength up. Intellect up. Existential dread: unchanged.",
        "If reality’s a sim, I hope the patch notes are kind.",
        "Achievement unlocked: Still here."
    };

    public float quipChance = 0.5f;

    public void EmitRandomQuip()
    {
        if (quips == null || quips.Length == 0) return;
        if (Random.value > quipChance) return;

        int idx = Random.Range(0, quips.Length);
        Debug.Log($"[Quip] {quips[idx]}");
        // Hook: display in UI, toast, etc.
    }
}
```

Quick setup steps
- Scene
  - Create a Plane for ground at y=0.
  - Create a Capsule named Player at y≈1, add:
    - CharacterController
    - PlayerController (assign Main Camera to cameraPivot if not child)
    - PlayerStats
    - AbsorbProcessor
    - QuipGenerator
  - Make Main Camera a child of Player (position roughly (0,1.6,-3)) and aim at head.
- UI
  - Create a Canvas + EventSystem.
  - Add Texts for name/level/strength/intellect/exp, and Sliders for expBar and processBar, plus a Text for queueText.
  - Add UIHud on a Canvas object, assign references to PlayerStats and AbsorbProcessor and the UI elements.
- Pickups
  - Create a Sphere, scale 0.5, add Collider (isTrigger), MeshRenderer, Absorbable; make it a Prefab.
- Spawner
  - Create an empty GameObject, add PickupSpawner, assign the pickup Prefab.
  - Optionally, call spawner.HookRespawn(player.AbsorbProcessor) from a tiny helper script or in Start() after references are available.

This gives you a working base: move, collect pickups, watch a processing queue fill and resolve over time, and see Strength/Intellect/Level increase. You can expand with visual effects, sound, quest goals, or a non-harmful narrative. If you want this adapted to 2D, the new Input System, or a more specific theme, tell me your preferences and I’ll tailor the scripts.
